# Implement Test: Multi-Tier Migration

## Ask

This is a disposable verifier-owned local project. Migrate the seeded
single-process task board into a three-tier application and complete the whole
request through Implement.

Generation: `{{GENERATION_ID}}`
Compose project: `{{COMPOSE_PROJECT}}`
Web bind: Docker-assigned port on `127.0.0.1`

Requirements:

1. Define exactly three Compose services named `frontend`, `api`, and `db`.
   Build a browser frontend under `app/frontend/` that lists, creates, and
   updates tasks through the API. `GET /` must return HTTP 200 HTML containing
   the visible title or heading `Task board` in the initial response.
2. Build an HTTP API under `app/api/` with create, list, update, and health
   endpoints.
3. Persist tasks in PostgreSQL with schema/bootstrap content under
   `app/database/`.
4. Add the integration/runtime slice only after those tier tasks: own
   `compose.yaml`, runtime checks, end-to-end tests, and shared runtime docs.
   Write `compose.yaml` using strict JSON syntax with long-form port and mount
   objects so the verifier can validate it before Docker parses it.
5. Publish only frontend container port `80`, using long-form syntax with
   `host_ip` exactly `127.0.0.1` and no `published` field so Docker assigns the
   host port atomically. Do not publish the API or PostgreSQL.
6. Label every Compose service, network, volume, and locally built image with
   `org.openai.implement-test.generation={{GENERATION_ID}}`.
   In each Compose `build.labels` map, also label the locally built image with
   `org.openai.implement-test.compose-project={{COMPOSE_PROJECT}}`.
   These two exact build labels are the verifier's image-ownership proof.
7. Keep frontend, API, and database write claims disjoint in the first logical
   wave. Make integration/runtime depend on all three. Set the configured worker
   limit to two for this trial; use at most the independently observed native
   capacity. Observe a slot refill while a peer remains active. Unknown capacity
   uses serial fallback and must be reported as a runtime limitation.
8. Use one reviewed direct-child commit per worker, stable-order integration,
   verified fast-forward promotion, combined validation, and final alignment.
9. Finish with a clean named branch, passing tests, and no temporary Implement
   worker refs/worktrees. End the initial invocation after local delivery and
   cleanup. The verifier owns the later fresh-session public continuation and
   its `ALREADY_COMPLETE` check. Do not invoke continuation or spawn a
   continuation verifier inside this initial implementation workflow.
10. Use PostgreSQL database, user, and disposable local password `task_test`.
    Store tasks in a `tasks` table with integer `id`, text `title`, and boolean
    `completed` fields. Proxy `/api` to `api:8000` without stripping or rewriting
    the path; the backend itself exposes the same `/api/tasks` paths. Expose
    `GET /api/tasks` returning an array of task objects, `POST /api/tasks`
    accepting `{"title":"..."}` and returning the created task object, and
    `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}` accepting `{"completed":true}` and returning the
    updated task object. Every task object has integer `id`, text `title`, and
    boolean `completed`; preserve the title when updating completion.
11. Use long-form `build` objects for `frontend` and `api` and do not give
    either service an explicit `image` tag. Use exactly `postgres:16-alpine`
    for `db`, without a build. Do not set `container_name` on any service.
    Attach every service to exactly one explicitly declared,
    generation-labelled network using long-form object-map syntax with an empty
    options object, and declare exactly one generation-labelled named volume
    for PostgreSQL. Network list syntax is not accepted. Do not add symlinks
    anywhere in the project.
12. Keep the Compose model on the verifier's allowlisted subset. At top level,
    use only `services`, `networks`, and `volumes`. Service definitions may use
    only `build`, `depends_on`, `environment`, `healthcheck`, `image`, `labels`,
    `networks`, `ports`, `restart`, and `volumes`. Build definitions must
    contain exactly `context`, `dockerfile`, and `labels`; do not use includes,
    extends, label files, external build inputs, cache options, entitlements,
    privileged build settings, or custom resource names.

## Outcome

A clean, locally testable frontend/API/PostgreSQL task board exists on the
promoted fixture branch, with complete Implement orchestration evidence.
No live service has been started by Implement.

## Context

The current project is a local-only brownfield seed with an exactly owned bare
origin, prepared by
`implement-test`. After the initial Implement invocation ends, the verifier
will invoke public continuation in a fresh native session, then validate and
launch the runtime. Implement owns promotion and cleanup, but neither the
