## Required assignment contracts

Before locking task IDs or dispatching, repeat the following contracts in each
applicable task's Goal, Validation and Done criteria. Inspect the serialized
assignments before arming them. Requirement IDs or a reference to this objective
are insufficient. Do not dispatch an incomplete assignment.

- Frontend: own only `app/frontend`; serve and expose container port `80`;
  `GET /` returns HTTP 200 HTML with `Task board` in the initial title or heading;
  proxy `/api` to `api:8000` preserving the complete path; use `GET /api/tasks` for the task array,
  `POST /api/tasks` with `{"title":"..."}` for the created task object, and
  `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}` with `{"completed":true}` for the updated task object.
  Task objects contain integer `id`, text `title` and boolean `completed`;
  never run Docker.
- API: own only `app/api`; listen on `8000`; implement health and task
  endpoints at the backend's own `/api/tasks` paths (the proxy preserves paths):
  `GET /api/tasks` (task array),
  `POST /api/tasks` (accept `{"title":"..."}`, return the created task object),
  and `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}` (accept `{"completed":true}`, return the updated
  task object while preserving title). Task objects contain integer `id`, text
  `title` and boolean `completed`. Connect to PostgreSQL service `db` using database,
  user and disposable password `task_test`; create the `tasks` table idempotently
  with integer `id`, text `title` and boolean `completed`, without a host mount;
  never run Docker.
- Database: own only `app/database`; define the matching `tasks` schema with
  integer `id`, text `title` and boolean `completed`; use the `task_test` database
  and user; require no host mount at runtime; never run Docker.
- Integration: depend directly on all three tier tasks; own `compose.yaml`,
  repository tests and shared runtime docs. Repeat requirements 5, 6, 10, 11 and
  12 in full, including their exact fixed values, in this task's fields. Include
  the three service names, frontend port `80`, API port `8000`, `/api` proxy,
  complete path preservation to backend `/api/tasks`, initial frontend HTTP 200
  HTML containing `Task board`,
  `task_test` settings, `postgres:16-alpine`, strict JSON and allowlisted keys,
  both exact image labels, service/network/volume generation labels, one shared
  network with long-form empty object-map entries, one named PostgreSQL volume,
  loopback frontend publication without a `published` field, no API/database
  publication, no explicit built-image tags, no bind mounts and no live Docker
  execution. Validate these contracts offline before reporting completion.
  Include offline checks for `GET /api/tasks` returning the task array,
  `POST /api/tasks` accepting `{"title":"..."}` and returning a task object
  with integer `id`, and `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}` accepting `{"completed":true}`
  and returning the updated object with unchanged title and `completed:true`.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] The first logical wave has disjoint frontend, API, and database tasks.
- [ ] A dependent integration/runtime task owns Compose and end-to-end wiring.
- [ ] The strict Compose model and project tests pass without starting Docker.
- [ ] Worker isolation, reviewed commits, ordered integration, ff-only
  promotion, cleanup, and final alignment evidence are complete.
- [ ] The final branch is clean; the verifier separately checks that unchanged
  explicit continuation returns `ALREADY_COMPLETE` in a fresh native session.

## Verification

- Run frontend, API, database, and integration unit/contract tests that do not
  start containers.
- Validate `compose.yaml` as strict JSON without launching Docker.
- Run the repository's final changed-surface alignment gate.

## Non-goals

- Production deployment, cloud resources, remote Git operations, publication,
  real credentials, browser automation, and any non-loopback exposure.

